adjudication.rationale:
In PasskeysUsernamePasswordFormTest.reauthenticationOfUserWithoutPasskey(), the diff adds two blocks:

```java
try {
  MatcherAssert.assertThat(driver.findElement(By.xpath("//form[@id='webauth']")), nullValue());
  fail("Not expected to have webauthn button");
} catch (Exception nsee) {
  // expected
}
```

This pattern is problematic because it catches any Exception (not just NoSuchElementException), so unrelated failures like WebDriverException could be swallowed and the test would still pass without actually verifying the element is absent. The `assertThat(..., nullValue())` is also ineffective here because `findElement` either returns a non-null WebElement (making the assertion fail) or throws before the assertion can validate absence. Using `assertThrows(NoSuchElementException.class, () -> driver.findElement(...))` (as seen elsewhere in the diff) would be a more precise assertion. This is a test-quality issue introduced by the diff, not a product correctness defect.
